TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Surfaced by lane-b057 while fixing B070 and closing B067 (2026-08-31). Filed
as its own ticket on that lane's recommendation: it wants a structural change,
not a behavioural one, and folding it into either of those tickets would have
been scope creep.

## The shape

The codebase carries **two scope predicates and two enum predicates**, each
pair with a wider member and a narrower one, and **nothing in the types stops a
caller reaching for the wrong one**:

| pair | narrow / correct for acceptance | wide |
|---|---|---|
| scope | `GlobalScope::scope_exists` (workspace OR index) | `TypeIndex::scope_exists` (index only) |
| enum | `has_enum` (acceptance) | `canonical_enum_key` (canonicalisation) |

Both members of each pair are legitimate. The wide one is deliberately wider:
`canonical_enum_key` still carries the trailing-suffix match that `has_enum`
dropped for B007, because every other caller reaches it only AFTER the checker
has already accepted, so it is free to be permissive.

## Two real defects, one at each end

- **B070 (fixed).** `hover` reached `TypeIndex::source_reference_key`, whose
  scope test is the INDEX-ONLY `scope_exists` - which cannot know what a
  workspace declared. Hover was silent for `Foo::VirtualKey` with
  `namespace Foo {}` declared, though the checker accepts it. A missing
  feature.
- **A near-miss introduced while fixing it.** The first revision of that fix
  called `canonical_enum_key` UNGATED - using a canonicalizer as an acceptance
  test. Hover then answered for **24 spellings the checker rejects**, all
  `Plot::X` matching the real key `UI::Plot::X`, which the game refuses with
  `Namespace 'Plot' doesn't exist.` Caught before it left the lane by a sweep
  over all 1403 qualified spellings in `docs/probes/b052-qualified-enum-spellings.tsv`
  asserting a one-way law (hover answers => checker accepts): 24 leaks ungated,
  0 gated, 0 on a disabled-hunk baseline.

**Opposite ends of one family.** One surface reached the too-narrow scope
predicate; the other reached the too-wide enum predicate. The direction matters
differently at each end: a silent surface is a missing feature, a confident
wrong one is a lie.

## Why a ticket rather than a fix in place

Both live defects are fixed. What remains is that the next caller has nothing
stopping it repeating either mistake - the pairs are distinguished only by name
and by a doc comment. Candidate directions, none chosen:

- Make the wide members harder to reach by accident (private, or renamed to say
  what they are: `canonical_enum_key_assuming_accepted`).
- Give the acceptance question ONE entry point that composes correctly, and
  express the wide ones in terms of it.
- At minimum, a doc-comment contract on each wide member naming its precondition
  and pointing at the narrow one.

## Bounded, by search rather than by reasoning

lane-b057 checked exhaustively that no OTHER surface currently carries the
defect: `source_reference_key` has exactly one production caller
(`hover.rs:677`, fixed), and the index-only `scope_exists` has exactly one
(`global_scope.rs:494`, which is the correct workspace-OR-index composition).
B063 and B069 are adjacent but reach this area through different functions.

**So this is not urgent - there is no known live defect.** It is a latent shape
with two demonstrated instances in one day, in one file, by one author who
knew the area well.

## Test plan

The one-way-law sweep from B070 is the model and should be generalised: for
each pair, assert over the committed corpus that the permissive member is never
consulted where acceptance is the question. Keep the anti-vacuity floors that
sweep already has - on coverage AND on how many spellings the surface answers
for - and note that it reads no game verdict, so B068's batching defect cannot
reach it.

## Related

B070 (the fix), B067 (closed not-a-defect; its writeup carries the mechanism),
B007 (why `has_enum` dropped the trailing-suffix match), B063, B069.

## A third instance, found independently by another lane (2026-08-31)

lane-b060-b062 hit the same shape in `src/typecheck/checker.rs` while fixing
B060, in a different file, on a different ticket, hours after this one was
filed from `hover.rs`. Two independent discoveries is much stronger evidence
for the structural claim than either instance alone.

**Its instance: THREE predicates over "does this name a known type", each
omitting what the others carry.**

```
names_a_known_type = has_type || has_enum || workspace_declares_type
```

- `has_type` **drops enums by design**
- `has_enum` carries the asymmetric Nadeo/Core source-name rules that make
  `UI::Plot::Marker` resolvable at all
- `workspace_declares_type` carries workspace enums

**The defect it caused.** Round 7 of that lane's review found a lost true
positive: inside `namespace UI`, a property typed `Plot::Marker` (the Core enum
`UI::Plot::Marker`, written relatively) passed into a callback slot. Master
errored; the branch was silent. The cause was asking only ONE of the three
predicates, so a Core enum stayed an unknown `Named` and went neutral. Exactly
this ticket's shape: the caller reached for a predicate that could not see the
case, and nothing in the types objected.

**Its local fix is the right local move** - state the composition once and use
it in both branches of `resolve_value_type_name`, so a type name resolves the
same way everywhere. That does not close this ticket; it adds a third pair that
a future caller can still get wrong.

## Updated inventory

| question | members |
|---|---|
| is this a scope? | `GlobalScope::scope_exists` (workspace OR index) / `TypeIndex::scope_exists` (index only) |
| does this name an enum? | `has_enum` (acceptance) / `canonical_enum_key` (canonicalisation, wider) |
| does this name a known type? | `has_type` (no enums) / `has_enum` / `workspace_declares_type` |

Three questions, seven predicates, and **three demonstrated defects in one day**
- two shipped-and-fixed, one caught pre-merge - all from a caller picking a
member that could not see the case in front of it.

That changes the urgency assessment recorded above. This ticket was filed as a
latent shape with no known live defect. It still has none *outstanding*, but
the rate is now three in a day across two files and two authors who both knew
their areas well. The argument for acting is the rate, not any single instance.

## Fifth instance (2026-08-31) — found INSIDE a commit that was trying to avoid this ticket

lane-b057's reviewer caught a real defect in B063: a workspace type shadows an
external one of the same name, so a plugin declaring `class CSmPlayer {}` was
being offered the ENGINE class's injected methods.

- `is_external_derived_from` resolves a name against the index whether or not
  the workspace shadows it.
- `is_external_type` already existed, already checks `workspace_type_shadows`
  first, and was the right predicate.

Nothing was missing. The correct predicate was sitting there, already written,
already correct. The caller reached past it.

**This sharpens the ticket, and the sharpening is the point.** The lane was
actively reasoning about B079 while writing this code — it was being careful
not to DUPLICATE a predicate. It never checked whether the predicate it CHOSE
answered the question it was asking. So:

> Avoiding the duplication half of B079 gives no protection against the
> selection half.

That splits the ticket into two failure modes that need different fixes:

1. **Duplication** — the same rule written twice, drifting apart. Fixed by
   consolidating, and detectable by an agreement test.
2. **Selection** — several near-identical predicates, all correct, and the
   caller picks the one whose edge cases do not match its question. NOT fixed
   by consolidating (consolidation can even make it worse by adding another
   plausible name), and an agreement test cannot see it, because both
   predicates are behaving exactly as specified.

Mode 2 is the more dangerous one and it is what four of the five instances
actually are. A type system could catch it — that is what the title means by
"the type system cannot tell" — but only if the predicates stop returning bare
`bool`. Two predicates that both answer `bool` are interchangeable to the
compiler and distinguishable only by their names, which is precisely the
information a hurried caller does not read.

Whoever takes this: the deliverable is not "merge the predicates". It is to
make the wrong choice fail to compile, or to reduce the set so there is no
choice left to get wrong. Say which, and why, before writing code.

## Design decision (coordinator, 2026-08-31) — ACCEPTED, supersedes the Work and Test-plan sections

lane-b057's design call is accepted essentially as proposed. Recorded here
because the reasoning matters more than the conclusion.

### Accepted: reduce first, then encode one precondition

**Move 1 — reduction.** Demote `TypeIndex::scope_exists` and
`TypeIndex::source_reference_key` to `pub(crate)`; publish the type triple
(`has_type` / `has_enum` / `workspace_declares_type`) as a single composed
entry point and demote members where callers allow. Measured, production
callers only: one external caller each. Reduction is primary because four of
the five instances were selection among *reachable* predicates — if the wrong
member is not reachable, selection cannot happen — and privacy is a strictly
smaller change than type-encoding for the same guarantee.

**Compose, never merely add.** A consolidation that only introduces a new
composed name leaves four plausible names where there were three, which makes
selection worse. Every composition must be paired with demoting what it
composes.

**Move 2 — a witness type**, for the one pair where both members must stay
public:

```rust
pub struct Accepted(());  // constructible only by the acceptance check
pub fn accepts_enum(&self, q: &str) -> Option<Accepted>;
pub fn canonical_enum_key(&self, q: &str, _: Accepted) -> Option<String>;
```

Authorised because it is **zero behavioural change**: both external production
callers already perform the acceptance test (`resolver.rs:165` under
`scope.resolves(...)`, `hover.rs:188` under `has_enum(...)`). It converts
existing discipline into a compile error rather than imposing new discipline,
and the diff is a signature plus two call sites. Type-encoding earns its place
exactly where privacy cannot — both members publicly callable, and the
distinction is a *precondition*. That is one pair, not seven predicates.

### The finding that corrects the obvious fix

**Privatising `scope_exists` alone would NOT have prevented B070.** Hover never
called it; it reached the index-only scope test *through* `source_reference_key`,
which is separately public and embeds it. A private predicate behind a public
wrapper that embeds it is not private — the boundary was drawn around the name
rather than the capability. Both must be demoted; either alone accomplishes
nothing.

Generalised into `docs/verification-hazards.md`: when restricting access to a
function, enumerate what else *exposes its behaviour*, not just what else calls
it by name.

### Accepted: the test-plan correction

The ticket's original test plan said to generalise B070's one-way sweep. That
is right for the **duplication** mode and useless for **selection**, because
both predicates behave exactly as specified and no agreement test can see the
difference. Keep the sweep only where duplication actually exists (the B063
checker/builtins pair, until this ticket removes it), and do **not** present it
as coverage for selection. Shipping it as such would be a test that looks like
a guard and is not one.

### Split out

The fifth instance (`is_external_derived_from` vs `is_external_type`) is now
**B081**. Neither move reaches it — no visibility boundary to draw, no
precondition to encode; they simply answer different questions. It needs an
argument newtype, which is a different mechanism and 7 call sites in the
batch's most-contended file.

### Sequencing

Move 1's `typedb/index.rs` half is self-contained and is the authorised first
cut: two `pub(crate)` demotions plus one line in `global_scope.rs`, removing
the exact path B070 took. The rest of Move 1 and all of Move 2 wait on
lane-b060-b062's merge.

**Nothing here is urgent.** There is no outstanding live defect; the argument
for acting is the rate, not any instance. Do not let that licence a wider
refactor of predicate handling.


## Reframing: visibility is not a tool here (lane-b057, 2026-08-31)

The first cut approved for this ticket — `pub(crate)` demotions — **does not
work**, and the correction changes what this ticket is.

**This is one crate.** `typedb`, `typecheck` and `server` are sibling
top-level modules in `src/lib.rs`. So:

- `pub(crate)` on a `TypeIndex` method restricts nothing from `server::hover`.
- `pub(in crate::typecheck)` is **illegal** on an item in
  `crate::typedb::index`: that path must name an ancestor module.

The demotion would have compiled, passed the full suite, read in review as
real hardening, and closed nothing. It is the same failure shape as the
`source_reference_key` finding one level up: the boundary drawn around the
NAME rather than the CAPABILITY.

**Scale, so this is not re-proposed later.** Twelve files under `src/server/`
hold a `TypeIndex`; 28 function signatures take one directly. There is no
boundary to tighten.

**So the ticket is not "tighten visibility". It is: delete what can be
deleted, type-gate what cannot.**

- **Delete.** Where a wide predicate has few enough callers, remove it rather
  than hide it. `source_reference_key` had exactly one production reference
  and no internal callers, so it did not need a keyword — it needed to not
  exist. Done in Move 1.
- **Type-gate.** In a single crate, **witness and newtype types are the only
  category of mechanism that generalises**, because they bind regardless of
  module boundaries. That is why Move 2 (the `Accepted` witness on
  `canonical_enum_key`) is the primary general mechanism rather than the
  secondary one, and it is an independent argument for B081's newtype beyond
  "names failed a careful reader": the newtype is not merely stronger there,
  it is the only kind of fix that can bind at all.

**A crate split** would make visibility real — `typedb` as its own crate makes
`pub(crate)` meaningful. Deliberately NOT proposed for this ticket: a large
mechanical change contending with every open lane, to satisfy a ticket with no
outstanding live defect. If it is ever worth doing, it should be filed on its
own merits and B079 should not be cited as the reason.

## Move 1 result: the refactor was not behaviour-preserving on the first try

Worth recording because the failure is instructive about the pair this ticket
is named for. `source_reference_key` wrapped TWO things: a direct
spelling-to-key map with no scope test, and an ancestor walk gated on the
index-only `scope_exists`. Replacing it with the direct map plus a resolver
retry looked strictly wider and was not.

`canonical_enum_key` answers `None` when the spelling ALREADY IS the key —
true of a Nadeo module-level enum whose synthetic module happens to coincide
with a real Core scope, e.g. `Meta::EPlugSurfaceGameplayId`. Ten such
spellings silently stopped resolving. The corpus sweep failed and named all
ten.

The general point: **"None" from a canonicalizer means "already canonical",
not "not found"** — and a caller that reads it as absence loses exactly the
inputs that needed no work. That is the same confusion this ticket catalogues,
in the return value rather than in the choice of function.